{"assets":["/editorial-assets/7c97846ed6453f2670055ee18dcedb2e750f1b36731eeaa4f7361e16c9e7ea0c.png","/editorial-assets/b432c23d334e09047956d9416408ed84e7b9667078e9ccc415a7466093cfb2a5.png","/editorial-assets/2f8ae2d8bed489521d17c5411e36a4eb6b3a2cf65da9bea50cecfd75449e6593.png"],"contestId":"799","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"title":[{"kind":"text","text":"A: Carrot Cakes"}]},"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"799A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/799/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"799A - Carrot Cakes"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"One of possible solutions — to simply simulate described process. To make it we need two variables — "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". In them we will store the time when each of the ovens will become free. In the beginning "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" equals to 0 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" equals to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"After simulating the process we will get a time for which possible to make "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" cakes with 2 ovens (this time equals to maximum from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"). It is only left to compare this time with value ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1) / "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" — a time for which possible to make "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" cakes using only one oven."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"spoiler"},{"attrs":{"title":[{"kind":"text","text":"B: T-shirt buying"}]},"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"799B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/799/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"799B - T-shirt buying"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's store in three arrays t-shirts which have appropriate color. About each t-shirt is enough to store its index, but we will additionally store its cost. It is possible that one t-shirt will be in two arrays (if this t-shirt colorful)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then we need to sort t-shirts in each array in increasing order of cost. After that we will process buyers. Also for each array we will store the pointer to leftmost t-shirt which was not purchased yet."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For a new buyer we need to look on array with t-shirts which appropriate to his favorite color. Using appropriate pointer let's iterate to the right until there are t-shirts or until we not found unsold t-shirt (to this we can store one more array "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"used"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" with type bool — did we sell or no appropriate t-shirt). If there are no t-shirt with needed color — print -1. In the other case, print the cost of founded t-shirt and tag in array "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"used"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" that we sold this t-shirt."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"spoiler"},{"attrs":{"title":[{"kind":"text","text":"C: Fountains"}]},"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"799C"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/799/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"799C - Fountains"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There are three ways — Arkady builds one fountain for coins and other for diamonds, Arkady builds both fountains for coins and Arkady builds both fountains for diamonds. In the end we need to choose a way with maximum total beauty."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first way is simple — we need to choose one fountain of each type with maximum beauty on which is enough coins and diamonds which Arkady has. To make it we can easily iterate though all given fountains."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The other two ways are treated similarly to each other. Consider the way when Arkady builds both fountains for coins."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's write out all the fountains for a coins in a separate array. We will store them as pairs — cost and beauty. Then sort the array in ascending order of cost. Let Arkady will build fountains with cost "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" coins and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Additionally we need an array "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"maxB"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"maxB"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" equals to maximum fountain beauty on the prefix of sorted fountains for coins which ends in position "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The option when "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" equals to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" should be considered separately. It can be done in one iteration through the fountains — from fountains with same cost we need to choose two with maximum beauty and update the answer with the resulting value."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"It is only left case when "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let's brute the fountain with cost equals to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". After that, Arcady will have "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" coins. After that with help of binary search we need to find the fountains prefix which Arkay can build for the remaining coins. With help of array "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"maxB"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we can determine the maximum beauty of fountain which Arkady can build."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Similarly, the third way is solved when both fountains will be built for diamonds."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"spoiler"},{"attrs":{"title":[{"kind":"text","text":"D: Field expansion"}]},"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"799D"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/799/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"799D - Field expansion"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"At first let's check if rectangle can be placed on given field — if it is possible print 0."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the other case we need to expand the field. Let's sort all extensions in descending order and take only 34 from them — it is always enough based on given constraints. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"After that we need to calculate linear dynamic where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" equals to maximum width which we got for the length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let's iterate through extensions in sorted order and recalculate "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". For each length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we need to expand itself or to expand the width "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". To avoid overflow we will not store the width and length which more than 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"5"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". On each recalculate value of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we need to check if rectangle can be places on current field. If it is possible – we found the answer and we need to print the number of extensions which we already used."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"spoiler"},{"attrs":{"title":[{"kind":"text","text":"E: Aquarium decoration"}]},"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"799E"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/799/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"799E - Aquarium decoration"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's divide the decorations in four groups: that aren't liked by anybody (group 0), that are liked only by Masha (group 1), that are liked only by Arkady (group 2), that are liked by both (group 3). Sort each group by the cost. Then, it's obvious that in optimal solution we should take several (or 0) first elements in each group. Take a look at the group 3. It's easy to see that if we take "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" decorations from it, then we should take first "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" decorations from each of the groups 1 and 2. Let's call these decorations ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from the group 3 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from each of the groups 1 and 2) obligatory, and let's call the other decorations free. If the number of obligatory decorations is less than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", we need to take several cheapest free decorations. It's easy to construct an "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") solution then: for each "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" we can construct the answer described in linear time and then choose minimum among these answers."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"To speed up the algorithm, let's try all "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from the minimum possible (it's easy to get it from integers "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and groups 1 and 2 sizes), making transfers from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1. We should maintain the set of costs of free decorations, and let's also keep integer "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", which is the number of free decorations that we should add to the current answer, and also the sum of the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" cheapest free decorations. When we increase "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" by 1, in groups 1 and 2 the most expensive obligatory decoration becomes free (because the value "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" decreases by one 1). These decorations (if any) we should add to our free set. We should also add one obligatory decoration from the group 3, so, the number of free decorations could change by one. So, we need at most 2 operations of adding an integer to a set, and an operation that changes "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" with the corresponding change of the sum. We can perform these operations in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"log"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")), for example, using two std::set objects in С++ (or its analogues in other languages) one set for the smallest "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" integers and one for the others. The overall complexity to change "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 is now "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"log"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")), so the overall complexity is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"log"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"))."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"spoiler"},{"attrs":{"title":[{"kind":"text","text":"F: Beautiful fountains rows"}]},"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"799F"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/799/problem/F"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"799F - Beautiful fountains rows"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's describe an "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") solution first. Let's try all possible pairs ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") and check if they suit us. Let's try all values of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from 1 to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let garden ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") denote a garden with fountains from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and let's say that a garden bans a position "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for a fixed "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" if there is non-zero even number of fountains on the segment ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"], i.e. the pair ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") doesn't suit Ostin due to this garden."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now there are two types of gardens we want consider: gardens of the first type start have form ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", and gardens of the second type have form ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The gardens of the second type ban such positions equal to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 3, ..., and maybe "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 2, ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" if ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) is even. These positions do not depend on "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", we can keep an array that tells us how many gardens ban each position of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Initially, we add 1 to all such positions for each garden, and we remove that 1 when the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" passes beyond the left bound of that garden."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"R"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be the set of right ends of all gardens of the first type. These gardens ban positions "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 3, ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is bounded by the maximum element in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"R"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Also, if there is a value "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"R"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such that ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) is even, then all positions "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 2, ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" are banned as well. We're interested in the minimum value of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such that ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) is even, so, we can keep two separate sets "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"R"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"even"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"R"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"odd"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" for even and odd values, and look for the minimum in one of them."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"So, it's easy now to determine if a pair ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") is suitable or not. We can try all "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", check is there is at least one fountain on ["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] and add ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) to the answer if the pair is suitable."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's improve the solution to "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/7c97846ed6453f2670055ee18dcedb2e750f1b36731eeaa4f7361e16c9e7ea0c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". We can note that each operation that changes banned positions for the second type gardens is a range query over odd or even numbers. So we can handle it with segment tree with range queries. Similarly, the gardens of the first type reduce non-banned positions of each parity to some segment, so we can do a range query to count the number of non-banned positions in the two trees described. The overall complexity is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/7c97846ed6453f2670055ee18dcedb2e750f1b36731eeaa4f7361e16c9e7ea0c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"spoiler"},{"attrs":{"title":[{"kind":"text","text":"G: Cut the pie"}]},"children":[{"attrs":{"problemCode":"799G"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/799/problem/G"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"799G - Cut the pie"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's first prove that solution always exists. Define "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(α) as the difference between the area of the polygon part contained in the halfplane with polar angles in range [α, α + π] and the area of the polygon part contained in the halfplane with polar angles in range [α + π, α + 2π]. Note that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(0) =  - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(π), and since "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is a continuous function, it reaches zero somewhere on [0, π]."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The proof leads us to the concept of a solution. If we can compute "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(α) fast, we can do a binary search over α to find the root. Computing "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(α) consists of two parts. First, we should find the intersection points of the line with the polygon, it is a known problem which can be solved in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/b432c23d334e09047956d9416408ed84e7b9667078e9ccc415a7466093cfb2a5.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". Second, we should compute the area, and there we can use prefix sums for oriented triangle (the origin and a polygon edge) areas that we use to compute the area of the whole polygon. The only thing we have to add is the oriented area of three new segments, that is easy to compute. The overall complexity is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/2f8ae2d8bed489521d17c5411e36a4eb6b3a2cf65da9bea50cecfd75449e6593.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", where ε is the required precision. Note that the angle precision should be more accurate than the required area precision."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"}],"kind":"spoiler"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/51947"}